<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The three books we are reading hang together as a single argument, but each has its own question.</p>
<p><strong>Book I</strong> asks: what is the final end of human life? Aristotle argues that there must be some ultimate end we aim at for its own sake, and that this end is <em>eudaimonia</em>. He then argues, using the function argument, that eudaimonia is activity of the soul in accordance with virtue.</p>
<p><strong>Book II</strong> asks: what is a virtue, and how do we get one? Aristotle's answer is that virtues are stable dispositions of character, acquired by practice rather than by instruction, and that each virtue is a mean between two vices.</p>
<p><strong>Book III</strong> (we are reading chapters 1–5) asks: when are we responsible for what we do? Aristotle distinguishes voluntary from involuntary action, discusses how ignorance and compulsion excuse, and sets out his account of choice as deliberated desire.</p>
<p>Read each book slowly, but do not get stuck on the first difficulty. Aristotle often restates a point in different words a few paragraphs later. If chapter 2 is opaque, keep going: chapter 3 may clear it up.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
